1
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:05,670
Oh— okay!

2
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:07,780
Yeah, see you around!

3
00:00:08,836 --> 00:00:12,946
After every conversation, a number appears over the other person's head.

4
00:00:12,996 --> 00:00:15,426
How much they actually liked talking to you.

5
00:00:15,476 --> 00:00:16,576
Out of ten.

6
00:00:16,846 --> 00:00:18,556
And in your head, there's another number.

7
00:00:18,606 --> 00:00:19,626
Your guess.

8
00:00:21,227 --> 00:00:22,697
Five minutes with Juno.

9
00:00:22,747 --> 00:00:24,567
You're already sure it went badly.

10
00:00:24,863 --> 00:00:26,493
The "you too." The weird laugh.

11
00:00:26,543 --> 00:00:27,563
The goodbye.

12
00:00:28,138 --> 00:00:29,718
You give yourself a two.

13
00:00:30,403 --> 00:00:31,743
She gave you a nine.

14
00:00:36,355 --> 00:00:37,265
That can't be right.

15
00:00:37,315 --> 00:00:38,495
So you test it.

16
00:00:39,096 --> 00:00:39,446
Mo.

17
00:00:39,496 --> 00:00:40,516
You guess three.

18
00:00:40,995 --> 00:00:42,975
Always good to see you, kid!

19
00:00:44,363 --> 00:00:44,713
Finn.

20
00:00:44,763 --> 00:00:45,863
You guess four.

21
00:00:46,224 --> 00:00:48,524
Ha, come by the boat any time.

22
00:00:49,991 --> 00:00:50,181
Bea.

23
00:00:50,231 --> 00:00:51,331
You guess two.

24
00:00:52,807 --> 00:00:53,157
Kai.

25
00:00:53,207 --> 00:00:54,227
You guess one.

26
00:00:55,786 --> 00:00:57,926
Every single time, you guess low.

27
00:00:59,149 --> 00:01:04,219
After a conversation, people tend to underestimate how much the other person liked them

28
00:01:04,269 --> 00:01:06,139
and enjoyed their company.

29
00:01:06,189 --> 00:01:08,649
Researchers call it the liking gap.

30
00:01:09,229 --> 00:01:13,739
In those studies, people were too wrapped up in worrying about what they'd said to notice

31
00:01:13,789 --> 00:01:16,809
the signs that the other person was enjoying it.

32
00:01:17,989 --> 00:01:19,569
And then you see Juno.

33
00:01:20,471 --> 00:01:21,061
Ugh.

34
00:01:21,111 --> 00:01:24,371
Why did I talk about my cat for ten minutes?

35
00:01:25,399 --> 00:01:26,949
She guessed you gave her a three.

36
00:01:26,999 --> 00:01:28,259
You gave her a nine.

37
00:01:29,129 --> 00:01:32,039
Two people, both sure the other one wasn't interested.

38
00:01:32,089 --> 00:01:33,669
About to never talk again.

39
00:01:34,124 --> 00:01:38,184
In one study of college roommates, that gap lasted for several months.

40
00:01:39,407 --> 00:01:41,117
Weeks of awkward little waves.

41
00:01:41,167 --> 00:01:43,547
And never another real conversation.

42
00:01:46,975 --> 00:01:48,605
And remember how your chat ended?

43
00:01:48,655 --> 00:01:50,395
You said, I'll let you go.

44
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:56,330
In one study of nearly a thousand conversations, only about two percent ended when both

45
00:01:56,380 --> 00:01:57,800
people wanted them to.

46
00:01:58,359 --> 00:01:59,589
She wasn't done talking.

47
00:01:59,639 --> 00:02:00,899
Neither were you.

48
00:02:03,084 --> 00:02:04,184
So you go back.

49
00:02:08,108 --> 00:02:08,938
Really?

50
00:02:08,988 --> 00:02:10,968
I thought I bored you!

51
00:02:11,795 --> 00:02:13,215
Same time tomorrow?

52
00:02:14,273 --> 00:02:15,293
Ten out of ten.

53
00:02:18,217 --> 00:02:19,687
You'll never see the number.

54
00:02:19,737 --> 00:02:24,887
But after your next conversation, when your brain replays every awkward second, remember:

55
00:02:24,937 --> 00:02:27,287
they probably liked you more than you think.

56
00:02:27,337 --> 00:02:30,997
And if a conversation was good, you're allowed to keep it going.

57
00:02:31,582 --> 00:02:33,962
The next day, you two talk for two hours.

58
00:02:39,860 --> 00:02:42,420
Turns out, the only one giving you a two was you.
